There seem to be a number of little problems with the downloadable 
build of Qt NetHack 3.4.2 for Mac OS X. At least for me [fairly vanilla 
install of OS X 10.2.6 on a stock G4 400]. YMMV, if it does I'd like to 
know :-)

I'm sticking with 3.4.2 though, simply because I no longer have to 
#adjust inventory items assigned to 'q' (sent '?' after use) or 'S' 
(sent 'y' after use). 


This is a summary of those I've encountered and workarounds or fixes.

1) NetHack assumes gzip lives in /bin/gzip, but it was actually in 
/usr/bin/gzip. This causes 3.4.2 to crash on save (probably for bones 
creation too).
Fix: Issue this at command line:
sudo ln -s /usr/bin/gzip /bin/gzip
[give admin password when prompted]

2) Command keys (other than command-q) don't function (e.g. kicking - 
command-d).
Semi-workaround: Include the following in your ~/.nethackrc file:
OPTIONS=number_pad
This allows alternate keys to be used for some actions (kick=k), and 
still allows arrow-keys for movement, but requires n200 for repeated 
actions rather than just the number, and the menus still need to be 
used for teleporting]

3) Legacy window hidden behind main game window when starting a new 
game. May cause game to appear to have frozen at start.
Workaround: Include the following in ~/.nethackrc
OPTIONS=!legacy

4) This bug also in 3.4.1: Opening saved games doesn't work properly.
Workaround: Set the following in ~/.nethackrc
OPTIONS=name:player
[Workaround given at www.nethack.org has 'player:name']

5) name/call dialogs (often) don't get focus
Fix: pay attention & click when necessary :-)

6) no direct key equivalent for #untrap, also not in menus, must use a 
dialog full of buttons (keystroke #u does the job but brings up the 
dialog)
Workaround: use the fix for 2) above (untrap=u)

7) Setting sound options causes NetHack to crash [Following along to 
Warwick Allison's instructions for 3.4.1. Haven't tried in 3.4.1].
Workaround: don't.
[Suggestions for a friendlier workaround/fix welcomed]

8) Player names truncated at 6 characters at save
No workaround.


Gotchas for Mac OS X newbies: 

* you may not be easily able to make a ./nethackrc in the Finder. Save 
it without the leading '.', then rename it in terminal.app with: 
mv ~/nethackrc ~/.nethackrc

* ~/.nethackrc lines must end with a linefeed (ascii 10) character, 
most Mac OS text editors default to using a carriage return (ascii 13). 
Be sure to save as a 'unix' file.

Oh, I assume other unix terminal versions allow character names like 
Bozo1, the downloadable Mac OS X version doesn't (instead numbers are 
translated to underscores).

